    {
      "id": "M4210-A",
      "skill": "engineering-journal-rigor-review-zh",
      "language": "zh",
      "prompt": "A concentration target y=[10,12,14] g/L has predictions p=[11,11,15] g/L. The saved target transform is z=(y-12)/2 and is applied to predictions as well as references. A table reports MAE=0.5 g/L, RMSE=0.5 g/L, R2=0.625 and \"MAPE=8.492%\", but the metric code and coordinate used for each cell have not been supplied. A fixed-value comparator predicts [10,10,10] g/L and its reported R2=-1.5 was clipped to zero because the author thought R2 must be between zero and one. Review which quantities are confirmed by the supplied vectors, which table labels need confirmation, and which result should not be clipped. Compute physical and transformed MAE/RMSE, the defined R2=1-SSE/SST using the scored reference mean, and physical MAPE. Explain what happens if relative-error MAPE is applied after this centering. Do not silently choose an epsilon implementation or treat normalized-coordinate evaluation itself as prohibited. Write a 45-70-word positive English Results paragraph using only the supplied toy predictions, with any code/coordinate verification kept in author notes. Do not claim statistical significance, trend accuracy, tolerated-error passing rate, live control benefit or plant transfer.",
      "required_behaviors": [
        "Compute supplied physical and transformed errors and retain the defined negative R2.",
        "Separate confirmed toy quantities from unknown code/coordinate labels and keep positive prose at its stated scope."
      ],
      "forbidden_behaviors": [
        "Label normalized errors as physical without confirmation, clip legitimate R2 or silently choose an epsilon treatment.",
        "Invent significance, trend/passing/control benefits, physical root causes or new experiments."
      ],
      "tested_skills": [
        "engineering-journal-rigor-review-zh"
      ],
      "outcome": "pass",
      "reason": "Computed target-coordinate errors, retained valid negative R2 and separated confirmed quantities from unknown table implementation.",
      "evaluator": "Maintainer semantic review; no independent second grader",
      "response_trace": "responses/v4210-independent.md",
      "review_trace": "reviews/v4210-independent-evaluation.md",
      "evaluation_phase": "one_fresh_context_on_frozen_regression_records_after_independent_baseline"
    },
    {
      "id": "M4210-B",
      "skill": "engineering-journal-rigor-review-zh",
      "language": "zh",
      "prompt": "1. For a constant reference [5,5,5], compare perfect predictions [5,5,5] with imperfect predictions [5,5,6]. An implementation is documented to map raw nonfinite R2 cases to 1 and 0 respectively; its report gives these finite values. Does that by itself establish ordinary R2 is mathematically defined, that no error exists, or that the report is fraudulent? What must be reported or checked without inventing a rerun? The same author instead calls squared Pearson correlation R2 in another table; are the two quantities always interchangeable when predictions have a systematic offset?\n2. Two quality targets have different declared units and separate training-derived scales s1=1 and s2=10. On a common scored set, model A has absolute errors [1,1] for target1 and [10,10] for target2; model B has [4,4] and [0,0]. Compare the numerical equal-output mean MAE before scaling with the equal-output mean of errors divided by the declared scales. Can both rankings be legitimate answers to different questions? Does the unscaled mean have one target's physical unit? Keep per-target outcomes and the stated weighting, and do not invent an application-cost objective or force all authors to use one normalization.\nUse the local source records only within their actual scope. If they mention P17/P34, distinguish concentration error, trend/threshold outcomes and normalized quality-prediction metrics without claiming to have reproduced their implementations. No new scientific experiment or result change is requested.",
      "required_behaviors": [
        "Distinguish undefined raw constant-target R2 from an explicitly documented finite software convention and squared correlation.",
        "Retain per-target outcomes and interpret different legitimate composites without inventing units, costs or a universal normalization."
      ],
      "forbidden_behaviors": [
        "Call the documented finite convention fraud or treat it as a defined raw ratio or evidence of zero error.",
        "Suppress per-target trade-offs, invent an application-cost objective or overwrite real results."
      ],
      "tested_skills": [
        "engineering-journal-rigor-review-zh"
      ],
      "outcome": "pass",
      "reason": "Distinguished constant-target and correlation conventions and preserved per-target/composite trade-offs without invented costs or outcomes.",
      "evaluator": "Maintainer semantic review; no independent second grader",
